meta {
  name: staff-stream-handshake
  type: http
  seq: 11
  tags: [
    manual
  ]
}

get {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/admin/v1/stream?token={{access_token}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Upgrade: websocket
  Connection: keep-alive, Upgrade
  Sec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==
  Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13
  Origin: http://localhost:3001
}

tests {
  test("the upgrade is accepted", function () {
    // 101 Switching Protocols. Bruno cannot speak the WebSocket protocol after this point, but the
    // handshake is the part worth testing -- it is where authentication and origin checking happen.
    expect(res.status).to.equal(101)
  })
}

docs {
  > **Tagged `manual`. Excluded from automated runs.**
  >
  > A successful upgrade answers `101 Switching Protocols` and leaves the connection upgraded, which
  > silently halts Bruno's HTTP runner -- every request after it is skipped, with no error and a
  > zero exit code. So CI runs `--exclude-tags manual`, and this request is for driving by hand.

  # Staff realtime stream (handshake only)

Bruno is an HTTP client, so it cannot hold a WebSocket open. The **handshake** is still worth
exercising, because that is where authentication and the origin check happen -- and a bug here is
invisible from the UI, which silently falls back to polling.

## Why the token is in the query string

A browser `WebSocket` cannot set request headers -- there is no API for it. So the token travels as
`?token=`, which is worse than a header (URLs reach proxy logs and history more readily). The server
therefore accepts a query token **only on a genuine upgrade request**, and it is why the request
logger omits query strings entirely.

## Three things to try

| Change | Expect |
|---|---|
| as written | **101** Switching Protocols |
| remove `?token=` | **401** -- the query fallback is not a way in without credentials |
| remove the `Upgrade` and `Connection` headers, keep `?token=` | **401** -- the weak path is gated on the request actually being an upgrade, so it cannot be used to authenticate an ordinary call |
| change `Origin` to `https://evil.example` | **403** -- checked against the same CORS allowlist, so a valid token from a foreign origin is still refused |

That third row is the one that matters. This exact bug shipped once: the middleware read only the
`Authorization` header, so the admin board could never connect and fell back to polling without
saying so.
}
